# PhishGuard - root .gitignore
#
# Goal: make it structurally hard for a real secret or a throwaway/OS file
# to ever end up in this repo, without hiding anything the deployed backend,
# the Chrome Web Store package, or the test suite actually needs.

# --- Secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Any real .env file, anywhere in the repo, at any depth. The one deliberate
# exception is backend/.env.example, which must stay tracked - it's the
# placeholder template contributors and Render's setup docs point to, and it
# is why "!.env.example" is required, not optional: `.env.*` alone would
# also match and hide it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- Real dependency installs ------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately scoped to backend/node_modules/ only - NOT a blanket
# "node_modules/" - because testing/shims/node_modules/ is a small,
# hand-written, intentionally-committed offline-test fixture (see its own
# files' header comments), not a real npm install. A blanket rule would
# silently hide that fixture from git too. backend/node_modules/ itself is
# always reproducible from backend/package-lock.json via `npm ci`.
backend/node_modules/

# --- Packaged/output artifacts ------------------------------------------------
# A built extension/checkpoint ZIP is a build output, never a source file.
*.zip

# --- Logs ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ignore stray/scratch logs by default, but keep the two disclosed,
# intentional test-evidence logs this project keeps on purpose as part of
# its own audit trail.
*.log
!testing/reports/*.log

# --- OS / editor junk ---------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/

# --- Scratch / temporary files -------------------------------------------------
*.tmp
*.swp
*~
